# ueventd rules for the bare-metal x86_64 PC target.
#
# GPT partition labels become /dev/block/by-name/* symlinks; that is handled
# by the kernel's EFI partition support plus ueventd's default block rules,
# not here.

# DRM/KMS node: privileged. Only SurfaceFlinger and drm_hwcomposer touch it,
# so keep it restricted to the graphics group.
/dev/dri/card0            0660   graphics   graphics

# DRM RENDER node: must be world-accessible (0666).
#
# Render nodes exist precisely so unprivileged processes can open them -- that
# is the whole distinction from card* nodes. Every app process runs minigbm's
# gralloc mapper in-process, and the mapper opens the render node to
# initialise. At 0660 root:graphics, DAC blocks apps (they are not in the
# graphics group), and the mapper fails with no SELinux denial to hint at it:
#     E <app>: Failed to importBuffer. Driver is uninitialized.
#     E GraphicBufferAllocator: Failed to allocate (1280 x 800) layerCount 1
#       format 1 usage b00: 5
#     E Surface: dequeueBuffer: IGraphicBufferProducer::requestBuffer failed: -22
#     F HWUI: drawRenderNode called on a context with no surface!
# which crash-loops launcher3, SystemUI and Settings while system services
# stay healthy. Matches device/google/cuttlefish/shared/config/ueventd.rc.
/dev/dri/renderD*         0666   system     graphics

# Input: evdev from USB HID, i2c-hid (laptop touchpads) and PS/2.
/dev/input/*              0660   root       input

# Audio: snd_hda_intel covers essentially all Intel and AMD PC audio.
/dev/snd/*                0660   system     audio

# V4L2 video nodes -- USB webcams, which is what a PC's camera actually is.
# The external camera HAL runs as cameraserver in group 'camera' and enumerates
# these by opening them, so it needs read/write. Hotplug is the normal case
# here (a webcam is plugged in whenever), which is exactly what ueventd rules
# handle and a one-shot chmod in init.rc would not.
# Matches device/google/cuttlefish/shared/config/ueventd.rc.
/dev/video*               0660   system     camera

# Kernel log. Default is 0600 root:root, which makes pc-kmsg-file (running as
# shell) ask for CAP_DAC_OVERRIDE -- and shell is an appdomain, which
# app_neverallows.te forbids any capability at all. Widening to the log group
# it already belongs to removes the need:
#     avc: denied { dac_override } comm="pc_kmsg_file.sh" scontext=u:r:shell:s0
/dev/kmsg                 0640   root       log

# Misc
/dev/rtc0                 0640   system     system
/dev/tty0                 0660   root       system
/dev/ttyS0                0660   root       system

# hvc0 is a virtio-console port carrying the logcat stream during bring-up
# (see init.pc_x86_64.rc and tools/run-qemu.sh). Writable by shell so the
# logcat-serial service can use it. virtio-console rather than a second UART
# because 16550 byte-at-a-time I/O is slow enough to trip Android's watchdog.
/dev/hvc1                 0660   root       shell

# hvc2 carries a PNG screenshot out of the guest during bring-up. Written by
# the pc-screencap service; the QEMU harness captures it to a file. This is
# the only way to see what Android actually drew while the virtio-gpu scanout
# limitation keeps the host framebuffer blank.
/dev/hvc2                 0660   root       shell
